Vaccination sharply reduces effective susceptibility: after 90% coverage with 95% leaky protection, remaining effective susceptibility is about \(0.1 + 0.9 \times 0.05 \approx 14.5\%\) of what it was among still-susceptible people. So the post-vaccination effective reproduction number is roughly \(1.8 \times 0.145 \times 4569/5000 \approx 0.24\), meaning the outbreak should quickly become subcritical. 

There are still 327 infectious people at day 20, so most new infections through day 40 should come from their remaining infectious time, plus a smaller second-generation tail. My median estimate is therefore around the low-to-mid 60s, with stochastic spread from transmission randomness and uncertain infectious-age composition.

