Brief reasoning:
On day 21 Riverton vaccinates 90% of its population with 95% efficacy against infection. This reduces the effective susceptible pool to about 14.5% of the population (roughly 725 people) and lowers the effective reproduction potential (R_eff) to around 0.35, given R0 ≈ 2.4. From day 21 to day 60, there will still be a sizeable but limited number of new infections generated by the 911 people infectious near day 20, but transmission decays as susceptibles (especially unvaccinated) are depleted and as many exposures hit vaccinated individuals with only a small chance of infection. A stochastic trajectory would thus yield a distribution of additional infections on the order of a few hundred up to roughly a thousand people, with the median around a few hundred to six hundred and substantial tail mass toward higher values in some replicates.

{"p10":220,"p25":420,"p50":660,"p75":900,"p90":1200}

Vaccination makes the remaining susceptible population only about \(0.1 + 0.9 \times 0.05 = 14.5\%\) as susceptible on average. With 3,871 never-infected people left at day 20, that is roughly 560 “fully susceptible equivalents,” so the post-vaccination effective reproduction number is well below 1. However, there are still 911 currently infectious people, mostly recently infected, so they should cause a noticeable but self-limiting tail of infections.

My median estimate is around 200 additional infections over days 21–40, with stochastic variation from contact randomness, infectious-period timing, and which susceptible people are vaccinated.

{"p10":160,"p25":180,"p50":200,"p75":222,"p90":245}

After vaccination, most remaining susceptibles are strongly protected: about 90% have only 5% of normal susceptibility, leaving an effective susceptible pool of roughly \(0.10 + 0.90 \times 0.05 \approx 14.5\%\) of the 3,871 still-uninfected people. With 911 infectious people at day 20, there should still be a noticeable burst of infections, but the intervention should push the effective reproduction number well below 1, so the outbreak should taper rather than continue explosively.

My forecast is centered around ~180 additional infections, with moderate stochastic spread.

{"p10":145,"p25":160,"p50":180,"p75":200,"p90":215}

There are 1,006 people still susceptible at day 20. With 90% vaccination at 95% leaky efficacy, roughly 100 remain fully susceptible and about 905 have 5% susceptibility. Because 3,389 people are currently infectious, the force of infection remains high for the next several days, so most unvaccinated susceptibles are likely infected, plus a modest fraction of vaccinated susceptibles. Secondary spread after vaccination should be limited because the effective susceptible pool is small.

My forecast is centered around about 150–160 additional infections, with uncertainty from stochastic contacts, vaccination allocation, and recovery timing.

{"p10":130,"p25":142,"p50":154,"p75":167,"p90":181}

After day 20, about 1,006 people remain susceptible. Vaccination of 90% with 95% efficacy leaves an “effective susceptible” pool of roughly 15% of that, but the 3,389 currently infectious people still create substantial near-term exposure. Most additional infections should come in the first ~10 days after vaccination, with limited secondary spread because the post-vaccination effective reproduction number is well below 1.

My forecast is centered around ~150 additional infections, with uncertainty from stochastic contacts, vaccine allocation among the remaining susceptible people, and infectious-duration timing.

