The vaccination makes transmission strongly subcritical: remaining susceptibles have average susceptibility about \(0.1 + 0.9 \times 0.05 = 0.145\), so \(R_{\mathrm{eff}}\) drops to roughly \(3.0 \times (2684/5000) \times 0.145 \approx 0.23\).  

Most future infections should therefore come from the large pool of people already infectious on day 20. Given the recent growth and ~10-day infectious period, I’d expect roughly 250–300 additional infections, with stochastic variation from vaccination allocation, contacts, and residual infectious durations.

