Vaccination makes the remaining susceptible pool much less infectable: about 10% remain fully susceptible and 90% have only 5% of normal susceptibility, so the effective susceptible pool drops to roughly \(0.145 \times 2684 \approx 390\). With ~1,944 infectious people at day 20, there should still be a sizable burst of infections over the next several days, but the post-vaccination effective reproduction number is well below 1, so transmission should decay rather than continue explosively.

My forecast is centered around the high 200s, with uncertainty from stochastic contacts, vaccination coverage among remaining susceptibles, and the hidden infection-age distribution at day 20.

{"p10":235,"p25":250,"p50":270,"p75":295,"p90":325}

At day 20, Riverton has about 2,684 people still susceptible. The vaccination campaign should reduce the effective susceptible pool sharply: with 90% coverage and 95% leaky efficacy, the remaining effective susceptibility is roughly 10% + 5%×90% ≈ 14.5% of the susceptible population, or about 390 “fully susceptible equivalents.”

However, there are still 1,944 infectious people at day 20, so even with vaccination, substantial spillover infections are likely before the epidemic dies out. I’d expect a few hundred additional infections, with uncertainty from stochastic transmission, vaccination allocation, and residual infectious durations.

